  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nCurrently, a deep trough is situated over the Great Lakes region \nwith weak ridging to our southwest. A short wave extends across the \nCalifornia coast and will traverse eastward through the day today. \nWith the surface high located to our east, southerly winds continue \nto dominate the region. As the short wave approaches from the west, \na surface low will deepen over the Rockies. This will lead to a \ntightening pressure gradient over the Plains and cause breezy \nconditions to develop this afternoon. Southerly winds around 15 mph \nwith gusts up to 25 mph will be possible for much of central and \nsouth-central Kansas. This strong southerly flow will lead to the \nfirst day in a stretch of above normal temperatures across the \narea. Afternoon highs in the middle to upper 80s are expected.\n\nWhile anomalous heights will lead to afternoon temperatures reaching \nabove normal through the end of the forecast period, the surface \ntrough associated with the shortwave will linger over central Kansas \nfor Wednesday and Thursday. Highs across south-central and southeast \nKansas are generally expected to reach into the middle to upper 80s \nwhile highs over central Kansas will remain in the upper 70s to \nlower 80s. The surface trough will retreat by the end of the week, \nleading to middle to upper 80s areawide for Fri-Sun. Overall, mostly \nclear skies, dry conditions, and seasonable warmth will persist \nthrough the upcoming weekend.\n\nThe next chance for a pattern change will come early next week as a \nshortwave kicks out over the Plains from the Desert Southwest. While \nconfidence in exact impacts remains low, continuity between model \nguidance suggests increased rain chances and cooler temperatures for \nthe tail end of the forecast time frame and beyond.",
